select public._seed_people($p$[
["albert-ekka","Albert Ekka","Lance Naik",null,"Army","14th Battalion, The Brigade of the Guards","14 GUARDS","1942-12-27","fell-in-action","1971-12-03","Jari","Gumla","Jharkhand","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Gangasagar, Agartala sector (Eastern front)","Lance Naik Albert Ekka, from the Oraon tribal community of Jharkhand, served with 14 Guards. On the night of 3 December 1971, his battalion attacked the fortified Pakistani position at Gangasagar, a railway station near Agartala, on the eastern front.\n\nHe charged an enemy bunker and bayoneted its crew, then went on to clear a second. A medium machine-gun firing from the upper storey of a building was holding up the advance; though wounded, he crept up, threw a grenade through the opening and silenced the gun before collapsing. He died of his wounds. Gangasagar was captured and the way opened for the advance into East Pakistan.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",null,true,"For silencing enemy light machine-gun posts with grenade and bayonet during the assault on Gangasagar, and, though grievously wounded, climbing to a fortified building to destroy a medium machine-gun that held up his company.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Lance Naik Albert Ekka, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~Td/d2/Lance_Naik_Albert_Ekka_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Lance_Naik_Albert_Ekka_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Lance_Naik_Albert_Ekka_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["kuldip-singh-chandpuri","Kuldip Singh Chandpuri","Major",null,"Army","23rd Battalion, The Punjab Regiment","23 PUNJAB","1940-11-22","survived","1971-12-04","Montgomery","Montgomery","Punjab","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Longewala post, Thar desert","Major Kuldip Singh Chandpuri commanded A Company of 23 Punjab at Longewala, an isolated post in the Thar desert. On the night of 4 December 1971, a Pakistani force of armour and infantry advanced on the post.\n\nChoosing to stay and fight rather than withdraw, his company held its ground through the night with anti-tank weapons, mines and mortars. At first light, Hawker Hunters of the Indian Air Force from Jaisalmer struck the column, and the attack collapsed with heavy losses of tanks and vehicles. He retired as a Brigadier and died in 2018.","GNA","Maha Vir Chakra",null,false,"For holding the Longewala post through the night of 4–5 December 1971 with a single company against a Pakistani armoured attack, until Indian Air Force aircraft arrived at first light.",null,["longewala-war-memorial"],null],
["mahendra-nath-mulla","Mahendra Nath Mulla","Captain",null,"Navy","INS Khukri, 14th Frigate Squadron, Indian Navy","INS KHUKRI","1926-05-15","fell-in-action","1971-12-09","Gorakhpur","Gorakhpur","Uttar Pradesh","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Arabian Sea, off Diu","Captain Mahendra Nath Mulla commanded the anti-submarine frigate INS Khukri, which was hunting a Pakistani submarine off the Kathiawar coast. On the night of 9 December 1971 Khukri was hit by torpedoes from PNS Hangor and sank within minutes.\n\nCaptain Mulla directed the abandonment of the ship, gave his own life-jacket to a sailor, and remained on the bridge as the ship went down. He was last seen there. Eighteen officers and 176 sailors died. He is the only Indian naval officer to have chosen to go down with his ship in war, and was awarded the Maha Vir Chakra posthumously.","GNV","Maha Vir Chakra",null,true,"For organising the rescue of his crew after INS Khukri was torpedoed, giving away his own life-jacket, and choosing to remain on the bridge as the ship sank.",null,["national-war-memorial","ins-khukri-memorial-diu","victory-at-sea-visakhapatnam"],null],
["nirmal-jit-singh-sekhon","Nirmal Jit Singh Sekhon","Flying Officer",null,"Air Force","No. 18 Squadron, Indian Air Force","No. 18 SQN","1943-07-17","fell-in-action","1971-12-14","Isewal","Ludhiana","Punjab","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Over Srinagar airfield","Flying Officer Nirmal Jit Singh Sekhon is the only member of the Indian Air Force to have been awarded the Param Vir Chakra. On the morning of 14 December 1971, Pakistani F-86 Sabres attacked Srinagar airfield while he was on readiness duty with No. 18 Squadron, the 'Flying Bullets'.\n\nHe took off in his Folland Gnat as bombs fell on the runway and engaged the Sabres alone, damaging at least two in a low-level dogfight. Outnumbered, his aircraft was hit and crashed. He was 28. His last radio call reported that he was 'behind a couple of Sabres' and would not let them get away.","GNI","Param Vir Chakra",null,true,"For taking off in a Gnat under attack by six enemy Sabres at Srinagar, engaging them single-handedly and hitting at least two before his own aircraft was shot down.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Flying Officer Nirmal Jit Singh Sekhon, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T9/9b/N_J_Sekhon_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-N_J_Sekhon_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:N_J_Sekhon_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["arun-khetarpal","Arun Khetarpal","Second Lieutenant",null,"Army","The Poona Horse (17 Horse)","17 HORSE","1950-10-14","fell-in-action","1971-12-16","Pune","Pune","Maharashtra","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Jarpal, Battle of Basantar, Shakargarh sector","Second Lieutenant Arun Khetarpal had been commissioned into the Poona Horse only six months before the war. On 16 December 1971, Pakistani armour launched a counter-attack against the Indian bridgehead across the Basantar river at Jarpal.\n\nRushing to reinforce the threatened squadron, he engaged the enemy tanks and destroyed several. When his tank, 'Famagusta', was hit and caught fire, he was ordered to abandon it but refused, saying his gun was still working. He destroyed another tank before a further hit killed him. He was 21. His tank is preserved at the Armoured Corps Centre at Ahmednagar.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",null,true,"For leading his tanks against a determined enemy armoured counter-attack at Jarpal, destroying several tanks, and refusing to abandon his burning tank, continuing to fight until it was hit again and he was killed.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Second Lieutenant Arun Khetarpal, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~Te/e3/Second_Lieutenant_Arun_Khetarpal_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Second_Lieutenant_Arun_Khetarpal_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Second_Lieutenant_Arun_Khetarpal_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["hoshiar-singh","Hoshiar Singh","Major",null,"Army","3rd Battalion, The Grenadiers","3 GRENADIERS","1936-05-05","survived","1971-12-15","Sisana","Sonipat","Haryana","indo-pak-war-1971",null,"Jarpal, Basantar river, Shakargarh sector","Major Hoshiar Singh commanded a company of 3 Grenadiers in the Battle of Basantar. On 15 December 1971 his company captured the village of Jarpal under heavy fire.\n\nOver the next two days, the enemy launched a series of counter-attacks with infantry and armour. Seriously wounded by shellfire, he refused evacuation, went from trench to trench to steady his men, and took over a machine-gun when its crew fell. The position held until the ceasefire. He survived, retired as a Colonel and died in 1998.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",null,false,"For capturing Jarpal and holding it against repeated counter-attacks over three days, moving among his men under heavy shelling and personally manning a machine-gun after its crew became casualties, though seriously wounded.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Major Hoshiar Singh, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T6/6d/Major_Hoshiar_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Major_Hoshiar_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Major_Hoshiar_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["bana-singh","Bana Singh","Naib Subedar",null,"Army","8th Battalion, The Jammu & Kashmir Light Infantry","8 JAK LI","1949-01-06","survived","1987-06-26","Kadyal","Jammu","Jammu & Kashmir","siachen-operation-meghdoot","Operation Rajiv","Quaid Post (Bana Post), Siachen Glacier","Naib Subedar Bana Singh led the final assault of Operation Rajiv, the operation to capture Quaid Post — a position on the Saltoro Ridge from which Pakistani troops overlooked Indian posts on the Siachen Glacier.\n\nOn 26 June 1987, after earlier attempts had failed, his team climbed a near-vertical 457-metre ice wall in a blizzard and approached unseen. They lobbed grenades into the post's bunker and cleared it in close-quarter fighting. The post was renamed Bana Post in his honour. He retired as an Honorary Captain.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1988,false,"For leading a small team up a sheer ice wall at about 6,500 metres in blizzard conditions to capture the enemy's Quaid Post, clearing it with grenades and bayonet.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Naib Subedar Bana Singh, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T0/06/Naib_Subedar_Bana_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Naib_Subedar_Bana_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Naib_Subedar_Bana_Singh_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["ramaswamy-parameswaran","Ramaswamy Parameswaran","Major",null,"Army","8th Battalion, The Mahar Regiment","8 MAHAR","1946-09-13","fell-in-action","1987-11-25","Bombay","Mumbai","Maharashtra","ipkf-operation-pawan","Operation Pawan","Kantharodai, Jaffna peninsula, Sri Lanka","Major Ramaswamy Parameswaran, whose family hailed from Kerala, was commissioned into the Mahar Regiment in 1972. With the Indian Peace Keeping Force in Sri Lanka, his battalion was conducting search operations in the Jaffna peninsula.\n\nOn the night of 25 November 1987, returning from a search near Kantharodai, his column was ambushed by LTTE militants. He led an outflanking move to encircle the attackers. In close combat he was shot in the chest; he wrested a rifle from a militant and killed him, and continued to direct his men until he died. The ambush was broken and several militants were killed.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1988,true,"For turning an ambush by militants into a counter-attack, closing in on the enemy and, though mortally wounded, snatching a rifle from a militant and killing him, inspiring his men to complete the action.",null,["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Major Ramaswamy Parameswaran, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T3/34/Major_R_Parameswaran_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Major_R_Parameswaran_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Major_R_Parameswaran_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["padmapani-acharya","Padmapani Acharya","Major",null,"Army","2nd Battalion, The Rajputana Rifles","2 RAJ RIF","1968-06-21","fell-in-action","1999-06-28","Hyderabad","Hyderabad","Telangana","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Lone Hill, Tololing complex, Dras","Major Padmapani Acharya, whose family hailed from Odisha, was a company commander with 2 Rajputana Rifles in the Dras sector. After the capture of Tololing, his battalion was tasked with clearing the adjoining features.\n\nOn the night of 28 June 1999, he led his company against a strongly held position on Lone Hill. Though grievously wounded by enemy fire, he refused to be evacuated and urged his men on until the position was taken. He died of his wounds. His daughter was born a few months later.","GNA","Maha Vir Chakra",1999,true,"For leading his company in an assault on a heavily defended enemy position near Tololing and pressing on after being badly wounded, until the objective was captured.",null,["national-war-memorial","kargil-war-memorial-dras"],null],
["manoj-kumar-pandey","Manoj Kumar Pandey","Lieutenant","IC-56959","Army","1st Battalion, 11 Gorkha Rifles","1/11 GR","1975-06-25","fell-in-action","1999-07-03","Rudha","Sitapur","Uttar Pradesh","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Khalubar, Batalik sector","Lieutenant Manoj Kumar Pandey of 1/11 Gorkha Rifles took part in a series of bold attacks during Operation Vijay, culminating in the assault on Khalubar ridge in the Batalik sector.\n\nOn the night of 2–3 July 1999, as his platoon came under fire from multiple bunkers, he led the clearing of the positions one after another. Wounded in the shoulder and legs, he pressed on to the last bunker and cleared it before he was hit in the head and killed. His actions secured a foothold on Khalubar, critical to the success of the operations in Batalik. He was 24.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1999,true,"Lieutenant Manoj Kumar Pandey displayed valiant leadership and courage of the highest order in clearing enemy positions at Khalubar in Batalik sector.","Gazette of India, 15 Aug 1999, No. 42-Pres/99",["national-war-memorial","kargil-war-memorial-dras"],["Bust of Lieutenant Manoj Kumar Pandey, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T2/27/Lieutenant_M_K_Pandey_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Lieutenant_M_K_Pandey_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Lieutenant_M_K_Pandey_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["yogendra-singh-yadav","Yogendra Singh Yadav","Grenadier","2690572","Army","18th Battalion, The Grenadiers","18 GRENADIERS","1980-05-10","survived","1999-07-04","Aurangabad Ahir","Bulandshahr","Uttar Pradesh","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Tiger Hill, Dras sector","Grenadier Yogendra Singh Yadav was part of the Ghatak commando platoon of 18 Grenadiers tasked with capturing bunkers on Tiger Hill. On the night of 3–4 July 1999, the team climbed a near-vertical rock face under fire.\n\nDespite multiple gunshot wounds, he continued to climb, crawled towards the enemy bunkers and silenced them with grenades and rifle fire, enabling the rest of his platoon to capture the position. Aged 19, he is the youngest recipient of the Param Vir Chakra. He continued to serve and retired as an Honorary Captain.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1999,false,"Grenadier Yogendra Singh Yadav displayed conspicuous bravery and determination in climbing Tiger Hill and capturing enemy positions despite multiple gunshot wounds.","Gazette of India, 15 Aug 1999, No. 42-Pres/99",["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Grenadier Yogendra Singh Yadav, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~Tf/ff/Grenadier_Yoginder_Singh_Yadav_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Grenadier_Yoginder_Singh_Yadav_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1502,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Grenadier_Yoginder_Singh_Yadav_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["sanjay-kumar","Sanjay Kumar","Rifleman","13760533","Army","13th Battalion, The Jammu & Kashmir Rifles","13 JAK RIF","1976-03-03","survived","1999-07-04","Kalol Bakain","Bilaspur","Himachal Pradesh","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Area Flat Top, Point 4875, Mushkoh Valley","Rifleman Sanjay Kumar volunteered to be the leading scout of the column that assaulted Area Flat Top on Point 4875 in the Mushkoh Valley on 4 July 1999.\n\nFacing heavy automatic fire from a bunker, he charged the position and killed its occupants in hand-to-hand combat despite being wounded twice. He then seized an enemy machine-gun and turned it on a second bunker, forcing the enemy to flee. His action enabled his company to capture Flat Top. He continued to serve and rose to the rank of Subedar.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1999,false,"Rifleman Sanjay Kumar displayed extraordinary valour and disregard for personal safety in capturing Area Flat Top in the Mushkoh Valley.","Gazette of India, 15 Aug 1999, No. 42-Pres/99",["national-war-memorial"],["Bust of Rifleman Sanjay Kumar, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T1/1b/Rifleman_Sanjay_Kumar_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Rifleman_Sanjay_Kumar_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1858,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Rifleman_Sanjay_Kumar_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["vikram-batra","Vikram Batra","Captain","IC-57556","Army","13th Battalion, The Jammu & Kashmir Rifles","13 JAK RIF","1974-09-09","fell-in-action","1999-07-07","Palampur","Kangra","Himachal Pradesh","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Point 4875, Mushkoh Valley","Captain Vikram Batra led one of the most arduous mountain warfare operations in Indian military history. On 20 June 1999 his company captured Point 5140 in the Dras sector without losing a man; his success signal, 'Yeh dil maange more', became the slogan of the war.\n\nIn early July his battalion moved to Point 4875 in the Mushkoh Valley. On the night of 7 July, while leading an assault to clear enemy positions on a narrow ridge, he went forward to rescue a wounded officer and was killed. The feature is now known as Batra Top. He was 24.","GNA","Param Vir Chakra",1999,true,"Captain Vikram Batra of the 13 Jammu and Kashmir Rifles displayed conspicuous gallantry, inspiring leadership and devotion to duty of the highest order during Operation Vijay in Kargil.","Gazette of India, 15 Aug 1999, No. 42-Pres/99",["national-war-memorial","kargil-war-memorial-dras"],["Bust of Captain Vikram Batra, Param Yodha Sthal, National War Memorial","~T0/0b/Captain_Vikram_Batra_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Captain_Vikram_Batra_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg",1600,1600,"Krishna Chaitanya Velaga","CC-BY-SA","CC BY-SA 4.0","~CFile:Captain_Vikram_Batra_statue_at_Param_Yodha_Sthal_Delhi.jpg"]],
["anuj-nayyar","Anuj Nayyar","Captain",null,"Army","17th Battalion, The Jat Regiment","17 JAT","1975-08-28","fell-in-action","1999-07-07","Delhi","New Delhi","Delhi","kargil-war-1999","Operation Vijay","Pimple Complex, Point 4875","Captain Anuj Nayyar of 17 Jat led a company in the attack on the Pimple Complex, a part of Point 4875 in the Mushkoh Valley, on the night of 6–7 July 1999.\n\nAfter his company commander was wounded, he took charge and personally cleared several bunkers, killing a number of the enemy. While assaulting the last bunker, a rocket-propelled grenade struck him and he was killed. The feature was captured. He was 24.","GNA","Maha Vir Chakra",1999,true,"For leading his company's assault on Point 4875, personally clearing several enemy bunkers, and continuing the attack until he was killed by an enemy grenade.",null,["national-war-memorial","kargil-war-memorial-dras"],null],
["sandeep-unnikrishnan","Sandeep Unnikrishnan","Major",null,"Army","51 Special Action Group, National Security Guard (7th Battalion, The Bihar Regiment)","51 SAG, NSG","1977-03-15","fell-in-action","2008-11-28","Kozhikode","Kozhikode","Kerala","mumbai-2008-operation-black-tornado","Operation Black Tornado","Taj Mahal Palace hotel, Mumbai","Major Sandeep Unnikrishnan was commissioned into 7 Bihar in 1999 and served in the Kargil sector before volunteering for the National Security Guard. Though born in Kozhikode, he grew up in Bengaluru.\n\nOn 27–28 November 2008, he led a team of 51 Special Action Group into the Taj Mahal Palace hotel. After a commando was injured, he arranged his evacuation and went after the terrorists himself, reportedly telling his men not to follow. He was killed in the exchange of fire. He was awarded the Ashoka Chakra, India's highest peacetime gallantry award, on 26 January 2009.","GNP","Ashoka Chakra",2009,true,"For leading his commando team inside the Taj Mahal Palace hotel, rescuing trapped guests, drawing the terrorists' fire to evacuate an injured colleague, and pursuing the attackers alone until he was killed.",null,["national-war-memorial"],null],
["mohit-sharma","Mohit Sharma","Major",null,"Army","1st Battalion, The Parachute Regiment (Special Forces)","1 PARA (SF)","1978-01-13","fell-in-action","2009-03-21","Rohtak","Rohtak","Haryana","jammu-kashmir-counter-insurgency",null,"Haphruda forest, Kupwara","Major Mohit Sharma had earlier served undercover in the Kashmir Valley, earning the Sena Medal for gallantry. On 21 March 2009, his team of 1 Para (Special Forces) made contact with a group of infiltrators in the Haphruda forest in Kupwara district.\n\nCaught in heavy fire from several directions, he crawled forward to drag two wounded soldiers to safety, then charged the terrorists, killing several at close quarters. He was hit in the chest and died. He was awarded the Ashoka Chakra posthumously.","GNP","Ashoka Chakra",null,true,"For leading his assault team against a group of heavily armed infiltrators in dense forest, rescuing two wounded comrades under fire, and killing several terrorists at close range before succumbing to his wounds.",null,["national-war-memorial"],null],
["hangpan-dada","Hangpan Dada","Havildar",null,"Army","35th Battalion, Rashtriya Rifles (4th Battalion, The Assam Regiment)","35 RR","1979-10-02","fell-in-action","2016-05-27","Borduria","Tirap","Arunachal Pradesh","jammu-kashmir-counter-insurgency",null,"Shamshabari range, Naugam sector, Kupwara","Havildar Hangpan Dada, from the Tirap district of Arunachal Pradesh, joined the Assam Regiment in 1997 and was serving with 35 Rashtriya Rifles in Kupwara.\n\nOn 27 May 2016, his team intercepted a group of infiltrators on the Shamshabari range at about 13,000 feet. Crawling forward under fire across rocky ground, he killed two terrorists and, though wounded, fought the third hand-to-hand and killed him before he died. He was awarded the Ashoka Chakra on Republic Day 2017.","GNP","Ashoka Chakra",2017,true,"For leading his team in intercepting a group of heavily armed infiltrators at high altitude, killing three of them in close combat, and continuing to fight after being wounded until he fell.",null,["national-war-memorial"],null],
["b-santosh-babu","B. Santosh Babu","Colonel",null,"Army","16th Battalion, The Bihar Regiment","16 BIHAR","1983-02-13","fell-in-action","2020-06-15","Suryapet","Suryapet","Telangana","galwan-valley-2020",null,"Galwan Valley, Eastern Ladakh","Colonel Bikumalla Santosh Babu, an alumnus of Sainik School Korukonda and the National Defence Academy, was commanding 16 Bihar in eastern Ladakh during the 2020 standoff.\n\nOn the evening of 15 June 2020, he led his troops to a point in the Galwan Valley to verify the removal of a Chinese post. A violent clash broke out and continued into the night; he was injured but continued to lead until he fell. Twenty Indian soldiers lost their lives. He was awarded the Maha Vir Chakra posthumously on Republic Day 2021.","GNP","Maha Vir Chakra",2021,true,"For leading his battalion in resisting an attempt by Chinese troops to establish an observation post on the Indian side of the Line of Actual Control, fighting at the front despite being injured until he was killed.",null,["national-war-memorial"],null]
]$p$::jsonb);

drop function public._seed_people(jsonb);
drop function public._seed_media(jsonb, uuid, uuid, uuid);
drop function public._seed_sources(text);